{"id":"boys-names","title":"Most common boys' names in the US","prompt":"Name the 10 most common names for baby boys born in the US from 1926 to 2025","noun":"boys' names","category":"Oddball","metric":"Rank, 1926-2025 (Social Security records)","lowerIsBetter":true,"source":{"name":"Wikipedia: Popularity of birth names for males (United States)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Popularity_of_birth_names_for_males_(United_States)","asOf":"2026-11-02","dataAsOf":"2025"},"answers":[{"rank":1,"name":"James","aliases":[],"value":"#1","n":1,"article":"James (given name)","fact":{"text":"It's the same name as Jacob, which turned into James via the Latin Iacomus and Old French.","source":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/James_(given_name)"}},{"rank":2,"name":"Michael","aliases":[],"value":"#2","n":2,"article":"Michael (given name)","fact":{"text":"It was the top US boys' name every year from 1954 to 1998, except 1960, when David beat it.","source":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Michael_(given_name)"}},{"rank":3,"name":"John","aliases":[],"value":"#3","n":3,"article":"John (given name)","fact":{"text":"Jack began as a nickname for John, and was the top boys' name in England and Wales every year from 1995 to 2005.","source":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_(given_name)"}},{"rank":4,"name":"Robert","aliases":[],"value":"#4","n":4,"article":"Robert (name)","fact":{"text":"Rupert is the same name: both come from old Germanic words for \"fame\" and \"bright\".","source":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robert"}},{"rank":5,"name":"David","aliases":[],"value":"#5","n":5,"article":"David (given name)","fact":{"text":"It means \"beloved\", from a Hebrew root that originally meant \"to boil\".","source":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/David_(name)"}},{"rank":6,"name":"William","aliases":[],"value":"#6","n":6,"article":"William (given name)","fact":{"text":"It joins old Germanic words for \"will\" and \"helmet\", and took off in England after the Norman conquest.","source":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William"}},{"rank":7,"name":"Richard","aliases":[],"value":"#7","n":7,"article":"Richard (name)","fact":{"text":"A top-five boys' name in the 1930s and 1940s, it has dropped out of the top 200 in the 2020s.","source":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Popularity_of_birth_names_for_males_(United_States)"}},{"rank":8,"name":"Joseph","aliases":[],"value":"#8","n":8,"article":"Joseph (given name)","fact":{"text":"Genesis explains it twice: \"God hath taken away my reproach\" and \"The LORD shall add to me another son\".","source":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joseph"}},{"rank":9,"name":"Thomas","aliases":[],"value":"#9","n":9,"article":"Thomas (given name)","fact":{"text":"It started as a nickname: Thomas comes from a Semitic word meaning \"twin\".","source":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thomas_(given_name)"}},{"rank":10,"name":"Christopher","aliases":[],"value":"#10","n":10,"article":"Christopher (given name)","fact":{"text":"Not even in the top 200 in the 1930s, it was the second most popular boys' name in the 1970s, 1980s and 1990s.","source":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Popularity_of_birth_names_for_males_(United_States)"}}],"extras":[{"rank":11,"name":"Charles","aliases":[],"value":"#11","n":11},{"rank":12,"name":"Daniel","aliases":[],"value":"#12","n":12},{"rank":13,"name":"Matthew","aliases":[],"value":"#13","n":13},{"rank":14,"name":"Anthony","aliases":[],"value":"#14","n":14},{"rank":15,"name":"Mark","aliases":[],"value":"#15","n":15},{"rank":16,"name":"Steven","aliases":[],"value":"#16","n":16},{"rank":17,"name":"Andrew","aliases":[],"value":"#17","n":17},{"rank":18,"name":"Donald","aliases":[],"value":"#18","n":18},{"rank":19,"name":"Joshua","aliases":[],"value":"#19","n":19},{"rank":20,"name":"Paul","aliases":[],"value":"#20","n":20},{"rank":21,"name":"Kevin","aliases":[],"value":"#21","n":21},{"rank":22,"name":"Kenneth","aliases":[],"value":"#22","n":22},{"rank":23,"name":"Brian","aliases":[],"value":"#23","n":23},{"rank":24,"name":"Ti